  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nOver the next week, we will keep the same general weather pattern in \nplace, which consists of troughs in the eastern Pac and eastern \nNorth America, with ridging across the center of the continent. \nThere will be some minor buckles to this pattern over the next week, \nwhich will usher in a couple of dry fropas, but otherwise it \nwill result in fairly prolonged period of benign weather conditions. \n\nAfter another frosty start, we have another beautiful November day \nout there, with southerly winds already ushering in warmer air, with \ntemperatures well on their way to the 50s, with some 60s expected \ndownwind of the Buffalo Ridge in western MN. These southerly winds \nhave developed ahead of a front that will move across the area \novernight. There's no no level moisture for this front to work with, \nso it will do no more than lead to a period of high cloud cover with \nwinds for Sunday switching over to the northwest. However, there \nwon't be much of an airmass change with these northwest winds, so \nhighs Sunday will be very similar to today, perhaps just a degree or \ntwo cooler.\n\nThe upcoming work week will bring a couple of frontal passages. The \nfirst will be another dry one with minimal airmass change on Monday, \nwith highs Monday and Tuesday expected to be similar to this \nweekend. The Next front arrives Tuesday night and is associated with \nan amplification of the central NOAM ridge and eastern NOAM trough. \nThis amplification will result in northerly flow for us for the \nsecond half of the work week, which will send temperatures back down \nclose to normal for Wednesday through Friday. This front will again \nbe lacking low level moisture, but there may be enough forcing to \nscare up some light rain Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, with \nabout half of the EPS members showing measurable precipitation (less \nthan 0.1\") at MSP then. \n\nFor next weekend, we get on the backside of the late week high, with \nhigh temperatures returning to at least the 50s. The warmth even \nlooks to take a step up to start the week of the 16th, with it \ncertainly looking like we're not done with highs in the 60s yet. \nEventually this trough/ridge/trough pattern starts to break down \nduring the week of the 16th, with our next shot at wet weather \nlooking to come during the window of the 18th to 20th. Until then, \nwe have 10-12 days of boring weather.",
